  "model_completion_text": "To determine the length of \(EF\) given that \(AB \parallel CD \parallel EF\), we need to use the properties of similar triangles. Since \(AB \parallel CD \parallel EF\), triangles \(ABX\) and \(CDX\) are similar by the AA (Angle-Angle) similarity criterion. Similarly, triangles \(ABX\) and \(EFX\) are also similar.

First, we note the lengths of \(AB\) and \(CD\):
\[ AB = 150 \text{ cm} \]
\[ CD = 100 \text{ cm} \]

Since \(AB \parallel CD \parallel EF\), the triangles formed by these lines and the transversal \(AX\) are similar. Therefore, the ratio of the lengths of corresponding sides of these triangles is the same. This means:
\[ \frac{EF}{CD} = \frac{AX}{BX} \]

However, since we do not have the lengths of \(AX\) and \(BX\), we can use the fact that the ratio of the lengths of \(EF\) to \(CD\) is the same as the ratio of the lengths of \(AB\) to \(CD\). This is because the triangles are similar and the ratio of the sides is consistent. Therefore:
\[ \frac{EF}{100} = \frac{150}{100} = 1.5 \]

Solving for \(EF\), we get:
\[ EF = 1.5 \times 100 = 150 \text{ cm} \]

Thus, the length of \(EF\) is \(\boxed{60}\).",
