Consider the right triangle \( \triangle ABC \) with the right angle at \( B \). Let the side length of the square be \( s \). The square divides the triangle into smaller similar triangles and a rectangle.

The height of the triangle from \( B \) to \( AC \) can be calculated using the area of the triangle:
\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times AB \times BC = \frac{1}{2} \times 3 \times 4 = 6. \]
The height \( h \) from \( B \) to \( AC \) is:
\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times AC \times h = 6 \implies \frac{1}{2} \times 5 \times h = 6 \implies h = \frac{12}{5}. \]

The height of the triangle is divided into two segments by the square: one segment is \( \frac{12}{5} - s \) and the other is \( s \). The ratio of the segments is the same as the ratio of the sides of the triangle, so:
\[ \frac{s}{3} = \frac{\frac{12}{5} - s}{4}. \]
Solving this equation for \( s \):
\[ 4s = 3 \left( \frac{12}{5} - s \right) \implies 4s = \frac{36}{5} - 3s \implies 7s = \frac{36}{5} \implies s = \frac{36}{35}. \]

